{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the Canaanite, king of Arad, who dwelt in the Negev, heard.</b> This is Amalek, as it is said (Numbers 13): ״עמלק יושב בארץ הנגב״, and he changed his language to speak in the Canaanite tongue, so that if Israel would pray to HaShem, blessed be He, to deliver the Canaanites into their hand, he would not be included among them. And behold, Israel guarded themselves against this, for they prayed in general terms: ״אם נתון תתן את העם הזה״ — 'If You will deliver this people' — and did not say 'the Canaanites.'" "<b>By way of the Atharim (האתרים).</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the spies (המרגלים). And the meaning is that when the spies came and went up into the Negev, the inhabitants of the land became aware of them, and this Canaanite who dwelt in the Negev heard about them and came by the way they had travelled until he reached the camp of Israel — and this is the view of the Targum." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״דרך האתרים״ — the way of the great spy (התייר הגדול), which is the Ark of the Covenant of HaShem that travelled before them a three days' journey to seek out a resting place for them. And some say it is Sihon king of the Amorites, and he is called a Canaanite because every Amorite is a Canaanite. And the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: what report did he hear? He heard that Aaron had died and the clouds of glory had withdrawn, and this is what is written: ״ויראו כל העדה כי גוע אהרן״. R. Abbahu said: do not read ויראו but וַיֵרָאוּ — for until now they were not visible to the nations, for they were concealed by the clouds of glory. And so in the Midrash Tanchuma: when Aaron died, the clouds of glory withdrew and they appeared like a woman whose hair is disheveled."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:27.588826612Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}